        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\dfrac{D_f}{c^2}=\\dfrac{D_g}{r^2}$.\n\nTranslations change the centre but preserve the constant in the centred product equation.",
          "worked": "Part (a) gives the centred equations\n$$\\left(x+\\frac{d}{c}\\right)\\left(y-\\frac{a}{c}\\right)=-\\frac{D_f}{c^2}$$\nand\n$$\\left(x+\\frac{s}{r}\\right)\\left(y-\\frac{p}{r}\\right)=-\\frac{D_g}{r^2},\\qquad D_g=ps-qr.$$\nTranslating each centre to the origin gives $XY=-\\frac{D_f}{c^2}$ and $XY=-\\frac{D_g}{r^2}$. These describe the same curve precisely when\n$$\\frac{D_f}{c^2}=\\frac{D_g}{r^2}.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 62
      },
      {
        "id": "p28-q50-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Determine if $y=\\dfrac{x}{2x+4}$ and $y=\\dfrac{x}{x+1}$ are congruent hyperbolas.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "They are congruent: $\\frac{D_f}{c^2}=\\frac{D_g}{r^2}=1$.\n\nCompare the determinant ratios using part (b).",
          "worked": "For $f(x)=\\frac{x}{(2x+4)}$ and $g(x)=\\frac{x}{(x+1)}$,\n$$\\begin{align*}\nD_f&=1\\times4-0\\times2=4\\\\\nD_g&=1\\times1-0\\times1=1\\\\\n\\frac{D_f}{c^2}&=\\frac{4}{2^2}=1\\\\\n\\frac{D_g}{r^2}&=\\frac{1}{1^2}=1.\n\\end{align*}$$\nThe ratios are equal, so the hyperbolas are congruent by part (b).",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 54
